\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Vision-language-action models (VLAs) fine-tune pretrained language models on robot demonstrations to produce actions from visual observations and language instructions~\cite{brohan2023_rt2,kim2024_openvla}.
In these decoder-only architectures, image tokens precede instruction tokens, and the causal attention mask prevents image tokens from attending to later tokens~\cite{wang2025seeing,pei2025rethinking}.
The readout token---the final position before action decoding---is the sole multimodal integration point.
How representations at this position differ between trained and untrained models, and whether the resulting patterns reflect training dynamics or inherited backbone structure, remains unknown.

Behavioral studies show that VLAs exhibit visual dominance~\cite{grant2026} and underweight instructions~\cite{haon2025}, but existing representational analyses target single architectures.
No prior work tests whether instruction routing mechanisms are consistent across backbone families or decomposes training-induced effects from inherited base-model priors.

We address this gap with a cross-architecture analysis of three VLAs---OpenVLA~\cite{kim2024_openvla} (Llama-2-7B), TracVLA~\cite{zheng2024tracevla} (Phi-3-mini), and NORA~\cite{hung2025nora} (Qwen2.5-VL-3B)---across four LIBERO~\cite{liu2023_libero} task suites (\S\ref{sec:setup}).
TracVLA and NORA baselines are pure VLMs ($N = 2$ independent VLM-to-VLA transitions); OpenVLA's baseline is the OXE-pretrained~\cite{padalkar2023_oxe} VLA (VLA-to-VLA transition).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.88\linewidth,height=2in,keepaspectratio]{figures/fig_teaser.pdf}
\vspace{-4pt}
\caption{\textbf{VLA fine-tuning reorganizes action-relevant representations in a pattern consistent across two VLM-to-VLA transitions but absent before action training.}
Training-induced effects ($R^2$ exit collapse, counterfactual sensitivity) are observed at the VLM-to-VLA transition, while L1 instruction attention reflects inherited base-model priors.}
\label{fig:teaser}
\end{figure}

Our analysis reveals three findings (Figure~\ref{fig:teaser}): (1)~$R^2$ exit collapse is observed in all 6 VLM-to-VLA cells, with an NLP control ($\Delta R^2 \approx 0$) consistent with VLA-specificity; (2)~all trained VLAs exhibit moderate to large counterfactual sensitivity ($d = 0.45$--$1.52$); and (3)~L1 instruction attention reflects base-model priors, not VLA training (\S\ref{sec:r2_collapse}--\ref{sec:l1_priors}).
Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}[nosep]
\item A cross-architecture representational analysis framework for VLAs, combining factorial trained/untrained controls with token-level probing across a $3 \times 4$ architecture-suite matrix.
\item Identification of $R^2$ exit collapse as a marker of VLM-to-VLA transitions, observed across two architectures and absent in NLP finetuning of the same backbone.
\item A decomposition of VLA instruction routing into training-induced effects and inherited base-model priors, grounded in cross-architecture trained/untrained contrasts.
\end{enumerate}

